#!/usr/bin/env bpftrace
// CASTLE: per-inode page-cache fill counter.
//
// Each mm_filemap_add_to_page_cache event represents a folio (kernel >=5.18)
// of 2^order pages being installed into the page cache. With BadgerDB v4.9.1
// the SST and vlog files are mmap'd, so disk reads surface here on page
// faults. We sum the page count per (s_dev, i_ino) and the post-processor
// joins inodes back to chaindata/*.sst and chaindata/*.vlog.
//
// Run with:
//   sudo bpftrace -o <log> ebpf_pagecache_ino.bt
// Send SIGINT to stop; END prints the @pages map.

// Note: BEGIN/END trigger probes skipped — bpftrace v0.14 on kernel 6.8 fails
// to resolve the BEGIN_trigger/END_trigger symbols (uprobe on /proc/self/exe).
// On SIGINT bpftrace automatically dumps all maps before exiting, which is
// exactly what we need. The shell wrapper records the start/end timestamps
// via [OBS] markers + iostat for cross-referencing.
//
// $1 is the kernel-encoded dev_t for the chaindata filesystem (passed by
// run_observation.sh). Filtering at the BPF level keeps the @pages map
// small enough to stay under BPFTRACE_MAP_KEYS_MAX and avoids competing
// for slots with irrelevant inodes (Go module cache, /tmp, etc.).

// No in-BPF dev or inode filter. Rationale:
//  1. chaindata lives on the same partition (nvme0n1p2) as /, /home, /usr,
//     /tmp, accounts_*.bin and the test binary itself, so dev filtering
//     gains little.
//  2. More importantly, bpftrace v0.14 "promotes" any args->X referenced in
//     a filter expression into the map key — adding /args->s_dev == X/
//     silently turns @pages into a 2-tuple-key map, which complicates the
//     parser AND inflates the key footprint.
// We instead raise BPFTRACE_MAP_KEYS_MAX in the shell wrapper and reject
// non-chaindata inodes post-hoc via inode_map.csv in the Python parser.

tracepoint:filemap:mm_filemap_add_to_page_cache {
    // Multi-page folio support: one event may install 2^order pages.
    // On kernels without folios `order` is still 0, so this is safe.
    @pages[args->i_ino] = sum((uint64)1 << args->order);
}
